course: biology
lesson: 35
title: Birds of every kind: penguins, ostriches, parrots, hummingbirds, songbirds, crows
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: About 11,000 kinds of birds share the same plan of feathers, beak and two wings, yet each has bent it to fit its own life.
explain: The penguin's wings became flippers, the ostrich's legs became racing legs with two toes, and the hummingbird's wings beat so fast it can hover and even fly backwards.
explain: Some birds are brainy: a crow drops walnuts on a road for cars to crack, a jay remembers where it buried thousands of acorns, and an African grey parrot can name colours.
explain: Others are tricksters: a cuckoo leaves its egg for a small reed warbler to raise, and a lyrebird copies a camera shutter and a chainsaw.
explain: Christmas cards get the map wrong: polar bears live in the far north and penguins in the south, so in the wild they never meet.
fact: Emperor penguin fathers hold the egg on their feet under a fold of belly skin for about two months without eating, near minus 40 degrees, huddling in thousands and taking turns in the warm middle.
fact: Polar bears live only in the Arctic and penguins live in the southern half of the world, so in the wild they never meet.
fact: The ostrich is the biggest living bird, up to about 2.7 m tall; it cannot fly but runs at about 70 km/h on feet with only two toes, and the head-in-sand story is a myth: to hide it lies flat with its neck on the ground.
fact: Parrots copy sounds with a voice box deep in the chest; Alex, an African grey studied for 30 years, learned over 100 words and could name colours, shapes and numbers.
fact: Hummingbirds beat their wings about 50 to 80 times a second in a figure-of-eight, so they can hover and fly backwards; the bee hummingbird of Cuba is the smallest bird.
fact: Crows in Japan drop walnuts at traffic lights for cars to crack, and crows can remember a human face for years.
fact: A jay can hide thousands of acorns in autumn and remember many of the spots in winter; the forgotten ones grow into oaks.
fact: The superb lyrebird of Australia copies almost any sound around it, including other birds, camera shutters, car alarms and chainsaws, and starlings in towns copy phone ringtones and alarms too.
fact: Flamingos are pink from the colours in the algae and brine shrimp they eat; chicks are grey, and the backward bend in the leg is the ankle, not the knee, which is hidden up in the feathers.
fact: Woodpeckers hammer wood up to about 20 times a second to find grubs, dig nests and drum to claim a territory.
fact: A cuckoo lays its egg in another bird's nest; the cuckoo chick pushes out the other eggs and the foster parents feed it even when it is far bigger than them; young cuckoos later fly to Africa alone, with no parents to show them the way.
fact: The kiwi of New Zealand is a flightless bird that comes out at night and finds worms by smell with nostrils at the tip of its long beak, which no other bird has.
exercise: Who keeps the emperor penguin egg warm in the Antarctic winter? | The father, on his feet under a fold of skin | The mother, in a warm nest of grass and moss on the rocks | Both parents, in a cave of ice | Nobody, the egg is buried in snow
exercise: Why do emperor penguins huddle in thousands and shuffle around? | So everyone gets a turn in the warm middle and less heat is lost | To hide from polar bears | To keep their eggs from rolling away down the slippery ice and breaking | To dance before they swim
exercise: A Christmas card shows a polar bear next to penguins. What is wrong? | Polar bears live in the Arctic and penguins in the south, so they never meet | Polar bears are too shy to stand next to penguins | Penguins only live in zoos | Nothing, they are neighbours on the ice and live side by side all year round
exercise: How fast can an ostrich run? | About 70 km/h on its long legs | About 7 km/h, a walking pace | About 300 km/h | It cannot run at all, only walk slowly
exercise: How many toes does an ostrich have on each foot? | Two | Four | Three | Five
exercise: What could the African grey parrot Alex do? | Name colours, shapes and numbers and use over 100 words | Read whole books aloud | Only say «hello» and «goodbye» and nothing else at all | Do long division in his head
exercise: What can a hummingbird do that almost no other bird can do well? | Hover and fly backwards | Swim under water | Run faster than a car | Sleep while flying upside down
exercise: How fast does a hummingbird beat its wings? | About 50 to 80 times a second | About once a second, like our heartbeat | About 5 times a minute | About 1,000 times a second
exercise: Why does a crow drop a walnut on a road? | So cars crack the hard shell for it | To hide it from other crows | Because it is too heavy to carry | To plant a walnut tree for later, as a store of food
exercise: What does a jay do with acorns in October? | Buries thousands and remembers where many are | Eats them all in one day and then goes hungry | Throws them at squirrels | Keeps them in its cheeks
exercise: How have jays helped Europe's forests? | Forgotten acorns grow into oak trees | They eat all the harmful oaks that make the forest dark | They carry water to the trees | They chase away woodcutters
exercise: What is the lyrebird famous for? | Copying almost any sound, even a chainsaw | Having the loudest song of any bird in the whole world | Singing only one note all its life | Playing music with sticks
exercise: Why are flamingos pink? | Colours in the algae and shrimp they eat | They are born pink and stay pink | They get sunburnt from standing in the lakes all day | They dye their feathers with mud
exercise: Why does a great spotted woodpecker drum on a dead tree in spring? | To claim its territory and find grubs | To sharpen its beak | Because it has a headache | To knock the tree down
exercise: Why does a reed warbler feed a cuckoo chick much bigger than itself? | The cuckoo laid its egg in the warbler's nest and the chick's open mouth makes it feed it | The warbler is its real mother | The cuckoo chick pays it with seeds | The warbler wants to make the cuckoo its friend, so that it will protect the nest from cats
exercise: What is special about a kiwi's beak? | Its nostrils are at the very tip, for smelling worms | It has rows of tiny teeth for biting and chewing worms | It is short like a sparrow's | It glows in the dark
test: lol ostriches bury their heads in sand when scared. everyone knows that | Myth: they lie flat with necks on the ground or turn their eggs | True, they hide their heads in the sand till the danger goes away | True, but only the males | True, they breathe through the sand
test: In an animated film penguins and a polar bear chat on an iceberg at the North Pole. How would you put it right? | Wild penguins live in the south and never meet polar bears | Penguins migrate all the way to the North Pole every summer to find food | Polar bears visit the South Pole in winter | Nothing is wrong
test: A zoo stops giving its flamingos their special food. What would happen? | They would slowly fade to white | They would turn green | They would grow bigger | Nothing, flamingos are always pink
test: My cousin says the flamingo's knee bends backwards. Is he right? | No, that bend is the ankle; the knee is hidden in the feathers | Yes, flamingos have backward knees | Yes, but only when standing on one leg | No, flamingos have no knees or ankles at all, their legs are one bone
test: What would happen if a hummingbird's wings beat only once a second? | It could not hover in front of a flower | It would fly backwards much faster than before | Nothing would change | It would hover even better
test: A crow keeps cawing at one man in the park every day. What is the likely reason? | Crows remember a face for years, and he once bothered it | Crows caw at every single person in the park in the same way | Crows cannot recognise people | The crow likes his hat colour
test: Your mate says a parrot just makes noises and can't mean anything. What would Alex the African grey say about that? | He could name colours and shapes, so some parrots understand words | He only copied the last word he heard | He could only whistle | He could talk about anything, just like a person, and tell stories
test: What would happen if all the jays in a wood vanished? | Fewer new oak trees would be planted | There would be more acorns in the trees forever | The oak trees would all die at once | Nothing at all
test: A reed warbler's eggs are pushed out of its own nest. Who did it? | The newly hatched cuckoo chick | The reed warbler itself | The wind | A woodpecker
test: How do young cuckoos find their way to Africa? | Alone, with no parents to show them | Their real parents lead them | The reed warblers fly with them | They hitch a ride on the backs of big geese
test: In a cartoon, a kiwi flies down from a tree to catch worms. What is wrong? | Kiwis cannot fly; they sniff out worms on the ground at night | Kiwis only eat fruit | Kiwis live only in the Arctic | Nothing, kiwis are great flyers
test: Crap, the phone rang again, no wait, it's the starling on the roof. Is that possible? | Yes, starlings copy ringtones and alarms | No, birds cannot copy machine sounds | Only parrots can do that | Only in Australia
exam: Identify the parent which incubates the egg of the emperor penguin during the Antarctic winter. | The male, upon his feet | The female, in a nest of stones | Both parents alternately each hour | Neither parent, the egg being buried
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the geographical relation between polar bears and penguins? | The former inhabit the Arctic, the latter the southern hemisphere, and they do not meet in the wild | Both inhabit the Antarctic | Both inhabit the Arctic | They share the same icy coasts in winter, the bears hunting the penguins for food
exam: Ascertain the cause of the pink colouration of the flamingo. | Pigments obtained from its diet of algae and brine shrimp | A pigment present from hatching irrespective of diet | Exposure to strong sunlight | The colour of the warm pink water in which it stands to feed all day
exam: It is given that an ostrich perceives danger. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning its conduct. | It may flee at speed or lie flat with its neck on the ground | It buries its head in the sand | It takes flight into the trees | It remains entirely motionless with its head raised
